<TopicPage slug="subjects/speech-therapy/foundational/speech-and-hearing-sciences">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Speech-language pathology rests on two sciences: how humans <strong>produce</strong> speech
    (anatomy in motion — lungs, larynx, tongue, lips) and how humans <strong>perceive</strong> it
    (the ear and the auditory brain). Every disorder a therapist treats is, at some level, a
    failure somewhere in those two chains, which is why the sciences come first in the
    curriculum: they are not background reading, they are the diagnostic instrument. A clinician
    who can trace a sound from diaphragm to cochlea can locate any breakdown along the way — and
    the location dictates the therapy.
  </p>
  <p>
    An analogy frames the whole page. Speech is a musical instrument played with the body: the
    lungs are the bellows, the vocal folds are the vibrating reed, and the throat-and-mouth tube
    is the resonating body that turns a buzz into a word. Like any instrument, it can fail at
    each station — a bellows leak (weak breath support), a damaged reed (a hoarse voice), a
    mis-shaped resonator (distorted vowels). And like any concert, it can fail in the listener's
    hall too: even a perfect instrument is wasted in a deafened ear. The "speech chain" — power,
    vibration, shaping, resonances, reception — is the map the therapist walks in every
    evaluation, and each stage below gets its own section.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from breath to meaning: the speech chain" widget="equationStep" config={speechCfg} />

  <h2>Anatomy of speech production: four subsystems</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Respiration is the power supply.</strong> Speech rides on exhaled air; quiet
    breathing moves about half a litre per breath at rest, but speech recruits more air and —
    crucially — controls its release against the resistance of the closed-ish larynx, holding
    subglottal pressure steady at roughly 5–10 cm of water for conversational loudness. The
    diaphragm and rib-cage muscles do posture-like work throughout an utterance, lengthening the
    exhale from its resting collapse into a metered supply. This is why breath support is the
    first thing assessed in voice disorders and why Parkinson's disease, which stiffens and
    weakens the respiratory musculature, typically presents as a fading, whispered voice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Phonation is the sound source.</strong> In the larynx, two small folds of muscle and
    mucous membrane (in adults, roughly 1.7–2.5 cm long) sit across the airway. Set them under
    just the right pressure and they snap shut and reopen in a rapid cycle — hundreds of times
    per second — riding on the airflow in a self-sustaining oscillation described by the
    myoelastic–aerodynamic theory: elastic recoil plus the Bernoulli suction that pulls the
    folds back together each cycle. The repetition rate is the <strong>fundamental frequency</strong>
    (F0): near 100 Hz for a typical adult male voice, 200 Hz for an adult female, 300 Hz and
    above for a child. Pitch is controlled by tension: the cricothyroid muscle rocks the
    cricoid cartilage to stretch the folds (raising pitch), the thyroarytenoids slacken and
    bulge them (lowering it). If the folds are apart or vibrating irregularly — paralysis,
    nodules, scarring — the voice breathes, hoarsens or vanishes, while all the articulators
    downstream remain perfectly intact: a source problem, not a speech problem, though the
    listener hears "bad speech" either way. Not every sound uses the source: <em>voiceless</em>
    consonants like /s/, /f/ and /t/ skip the buzz entirely and are made of pure turbulence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Resonation and articulation are the shaping.</strong> The velum (soft palate) is a
    valve: raised, it seals the nose off and directs all sound through the mouth; lowered, it
    couples the nasal cavities in, which is how /m/, /n/ and /ng/ get their hum — and how a
    cleft palate or an incompetent velum leaks air through the nose and smears every vowel with
    hypernasality. Below it, the articulators — tongue, lips, jaw, teeth, alveolar ridge, hard
    and soft palate — sculpt constrictions that classify every consonant by <strong>place</strong>
    (where the narrowing is: bilabial /p/, alveolar /t/, velar /k/), <strong>manner</strong>
    (how: complete stop, turbulent fricative, nasal) and <strong>voicing</strong> (whether the
    larynx buzzes during it: /p/ versus /b/). The tongue is the star of the ensemble — eight
    muscles, capable of extraordinary speed (speech articulators move many times per second) and
    the fine shape control that gives each vowel its acoustic signature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the formant peaks that define a vowel" widget="plotter" config={formantsCfg} />

  <h2>Acoustics of speech: the source–filter story</h2>
  <p>
    The physics that ties production to perception is <strong>source–filter theory</strong>
    (Gunnar Fant, 1960): the sound that leaves the lips is the glottal source <em>multiplied
    by</em> the vocal tract's filtering. The glottal buzz is rich in harmonics — energy at the
    fundamental and at every whole-number multiple of it. The vocal tract, a tube closed at one
    end, boosts some frequency regions and dampens others; the boosted regions are the
    <strong>formants</strong>, and because the tract's shape changes with every tongue and lip
    movement, the formants move with it. What the ear actually uses for vowel identity is mostly
    the first two: <strong>F1 falls as the tongue lowers and the jaw opens</strong> (so /i/ in
    "see" has low F1, around 300 Hz, while /a/ in "father" has high F1, around 700–800 Hz), and
    <strong>F2 falls as the tongue retracts</strong> (front vowels push F2 up toward 2,000 Hz and
    beyond; back vowels like /u/ drop it below 1,000 Hz). The plot above shows the formant peaks
    of a mid-back rounded vowel: a low F1 with a mid F2 is the acoustic fingerprint of a vowel
    like the one in "book". Say "ee" then "oo" slowly and you can feel F2 plummet — that is your
    tongue travelling backward. A vowel is, to first approximation, two numbers; the whole vowel
    system of a language is a map whose axes are F1 and F2.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consonants ride the same physics with different sources. Stops (/p/, /t/, /k/) are built
    from silence, a pressure burst, and the formant <em>transitions</em> before and after —
    whose direction and timing identify the place of articulation; the <strong>voice onset
    time</strong> (the gap between release and the buzz's return) separates /p/ from /b/,
    /t/ from /d/: voicing lag for the first pair, near-zero for the second. Fricatives (/s/,
    /ʃ/, /f/) are filtered turbulence — noise whose spectral peak (high for /s/, around
    4,000–8,000 Hz, low for /f/) marks the constriction's size. Spectrograms display all of it:
    dark horizontal stripes (formants), vertical strokes (bursts), grey fuzz (frication) — and
    speech therapy teaches clients to read this picture of their own voice, because seeing a
    formant move is a powerful way to learn to feel it move. Loudness, meanwhile, lives on the
    decibel scale, which is logarithmic: every 10 dB is roughly a doubling of perceived
    loudness, conversational speech sits near 60 dB SPL, a whisper near 30, and prolonged
    exposure above about 85 dB begins to injure the ear.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that different speech sounds happen because the vocal folds "make" each sound. The folds make essentially one thing — a buzzing source; the <em>shape</em> of the tract above them does the differentiating. This is why people can talk intelligibly after a laryngectomy (with an electrolarynx as source) and why a beautiful voice cannot rescue distorted vowels: the information lives in the filter, not the source.</p>

  <h2>Audiology basics: the audiogram as hearing's map</h2>
  <p>
    Hearing is tested by frequency and loudness, and the result is plotted as the
    <strong>audiogram</strong>: frequencies from low (left) to high (right) — 250 to 8,000 Hz is
    the standard clinical span — against threshold intensity running downward, in dB HL.
    Speech energy occupies roughly 250–4,000 Hz, but its <em>intelligibility</em> leans hard on
    the high frequencies, where the consonants /s/, /f/, /th/ and /k/ live; vowels, low and
    loud, are comparatively robust. That asymmetry explains the classic presentation of
    <strong>presbycusis</strong>, age-related loss, which typically slopes downward on the
    high-frequency side first: the patient reports that speech sounds muffled rather than quiet
    — "everyone mumbles" — because the vowels arrive and the consonants do not. The audiogram is
    the therapist's map of what the ear can and cannot deliver to the brain.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audiogram also separates the two great families of hearing loss. In
    <strong>conductive</strong> loss, the problem is mechanical — wax, fluid behind the eardrum
    (the otitis media of childhood), ossicle damage — and the treatment can be medical;
    classically, <em>bone conduction</em> (sound delivered by vibrator to the skull, bypassing
    the outer and middle ear) is preserved while air conduction is impaired, and the air–bone
    gap on the chart is the signature. In <strong>sensorineural</strong> loss, the cochlea's hair
    cells or the auditory nerve are damaged — noise exposure, ageing, ototoxic drugs — and both
    curves fall together; hair cells do not regenerate, so the loss is permanent and the
    management is amplification or prosthetics. Degrees are graded from normal (thresholds to
    about 25 dB HL) through mild, moderate, severe to profound (90 dB HL and beyond). Beyond the
    pure tones sit the working tests: tympanometry probes middle-ear status, otoacoustic
    emissions and auditory brainstem response screen newborns — the reason most congenital
    hearing loss is now identified in the first weeks of life, before language even starts.
    Interventions track the chain: hearing aids amplify where amplification helps (a damaged but
    present cochlea); a <strong>cochlear implant</strong> bypasses dead hair cells entirely,
    converting sound to electrical stimulation of the nerve — which is why candidacy depends on
    an intact auditory nerve and why the device delivers, at best, a coarse but genuinely
    functional signal that the brain learns to hear with.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the sciences matter clinically: chain analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Diagnosis in speech therapy is locating the broken link — and the therapy follows the link,
    not the label. Weak, fading loudness across all sounds points upstream to respiration and
    phonation (the Lee Silverman Voice Treatment approach for Parkinson's patients is,
    essentially, rebuilding subglottal pressure and loudness). Hypernasality plus nasal air
    escape points to the velopharyngeal valve — sometimes surgically, not therapeutically.
    Distorted vowels and inconsistent errors point to articulation or, when the errors are
    wildly inconsistent, to motor planning (apraxia of speech) rather than weakness. A child
    who cannot produce /s/ but hears the difference has an articulation problem; one who cannot
    <em>hear</em> the difference has a phonological one — and the two want entirely different
    therapies. And a flat, severe audiogram routes the case to audiology first, because no
    speech therapy can run on a signal the ear never delivers. The sciences are the diagnostic
    instrument; treatment is what you do once the instrument has spoken.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The cochlea repays the deepest look, because it is where physics becomes perception. A
    snail-shaped tube of fluid lined with about 15,000 hair cells is arranged
    <strong>tonotopically</strong>: the base responds to the highest frequencies (up to roughly
    20,000 Hz), the apex to the lowest — a mechanical frequency analyser built from a stiffness
    gradient in the basilar membrane, so different frequencies flex different places and excite
    different nerve fibres. Place coding handles the range; phase-locking of nerve firing adds
    precision at low frequencies; and the outer hair cells actively sharpen the tuning —
    literally injecting mechanical energy, which is where otoacoustic emissions (the ear's own
    faint echo) come from. The same tonotopy explains the audiogram's geometry: a noise-damaged
    cochlea dies from the base (high frequencies) inward, so noise-induced loss climbs the chart
    from the right. At the perception end, phonemes are <strong>categorical</strong>: a continuum
    of voice onset times between /p/ and /b/ is heard as two discrete sounds with a sharp
    boundary, not a blend — evidence that the auditory brain actively sorts the acoustic stream
    into phoneme categories, a skill tuned by language experience (the boundary sits in
    different places across languages) and lost, famously, for foreign contrasts in adulthood.
    Open questions run along this border: how much of speech perception is auditory versus
    motor (the motor theory's revival in mirror-neuron work remains contested), how the
    impaired cochlea's degraded signal can be re-tuned by the brain after implantation, and how
    hearing protection should be framed — the 85 dB / 8-hour industrial guideline with its
    3 dB exchange rate implies that every 3 dB louder halves the safe exposure time, a fact
    that makes the therapist's counselling about earphones and concerts genuinely quantitative.
    From diaphragm to decibel, the page's one map — the speech chain — holds: find the broken
    link, and you have found the case.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASHA/CAA accreditation standards; Ferrand, <em>Speech Science: An Introduction</em>; Zemlin, <em>Speech and Hearing Science</em>; Fant, <em>Acoustic Theory of Speech Production</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
